#
# prima-materia — the modern vipassanā movement, entered at three works of
# Mahāsi Sayādaw
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-modern.ttl with no node. The
# registration names Mahāsi's Manual of Insight and Ledi Sayādaw's manuals.
# What was opened is Mahāsi: the Pali treatise Visuddhiñāṇakathā in the
# bilingual Kandy print of 1965, read complete in the English and at every
# entered place in the Pali; the recorded discourse on the basic practice in
# U Pe Thin's English, read complete; and Practical Insight Meditation, which
# its own preface says is the English of chapter V of the Burmese Manual of
# Insight, read complete. The other chapters of the Manual and the whole of
# Ledi are unopened and the work nodes say so.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices; the stages in the order of the treatise; the prerequisites; the
# term; the conditions of failure; the cautions; the reworking.
#
# Citation. The treatise is cited by the page of Nyanaponika's English and
# by the page of the Pali text printed in the same volume (pp. 39-58). The
# printed page numbers stand at the foot of the page; the scan repeats page
# 21 once, and the text layer loses the numerals of pages 1, 5, 8, 11, 13,
# 19 and 25, which were restored from the sequence. The two English
# discourses are cited by the page of the Rangoon print.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Visuddhiñāṇakathā (The Progress of Insight)"@en, "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Visuddhiñāṇakathā (Der Fortschritt der Einsicht)"@de ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-10-22 in The Progress of Insight through the Stages of Purification, a modern Pali treatise on Buddhist Satipatthana meditation by the Venerable Mahasi Sayadaw, English translation with notes by Nyanaponika Thera, with the original Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), title page in the text layer of archive.org the-progress-of-insight-1965-pali-english-bilingual-version-mahasi-sayadaw.pdf. The English, pp. 1-26, was read complete with the translator's notes, pp. 27-32, and the short biography, pp. 34-35; the Pali, pp. 39-58, was read complete and compared at every place entered. The colophon in both languages says the author wrote the work in Burmese and rendered it into Pali himself; the English gives the date of composition as 22 May 1950. The Burmese version was not looked for."@en ;
    skos:note "One translator. No second translation of the treatise was found on archive.org under the English title, under Visuddhiñāṇakathā or under the author's name, and the later BPS reprints carry the same Nyanaponika text. The rule of two translations is met here by reading the author's own Pali at every place, which is the original and not a translation; where Nyanaponika departs from it, the node says so. The departures that matter are units of time: the Pali counts in minita and ghaṭikā, and Nyanaponika renders ghaṭikā as hour throughout, which fits the Burmese usage the author writes from, and once prints six minutes where the Pali has five."@en .

pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation (the discourse on the basic practice)"@en, "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation (die Unterweisung in der Grundübung)"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-22 in The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation: A Basic Buddhist Mindfulness Exercise, by the Venerable Mahasi Sayadaw (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979; first reprint 1996, second reprint 2003), pp. 1-34, title page in the text layer of archive.org TheSatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, which binds it with Practical Insight Meditation. The introduction says that the discourse was given daily from December 1949 to each batch of meditators arriving at the centre in Rangoon, recorded on tape on 27 July 1951 and played daily thereafter, printed in Burmese in 1954, and translated by the late U Pe Thin. Only the English was read; the Burmese was not looked for. The text layer drops the numerals of pages 1 to 9, which were counted from page 10."@en .

pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation (chapter V of the Manual of Insight)"@en, "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation (Kapitel V des Handbuchs der Einsicht)"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-22 in Practical Insight Meditation, by the Venerable Mahasi Sayadaw (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), the preface, parts I and II, the notes and the appendix, pp. 9-64, in the same scan as the discourse on the basic practice. The author's preface, dated 1 October 1970, says that the book is the English of chapter V of his Burmese treatise on insight meditation in two volumes, completed in 1944: its first fourteen pages translated by U Pe Thin in 1954 and revised by Mary McCollum, pages 15 to 51 translated by Myanaung U Tin, and the whole put into final shape by Nyanaponika. It also says that chapter V alone was written in common language without references to the texts. Two translators and one reviser stand behind one text, so the node citing it names one translation and not two."@en .

pmw:VipassanaShuniKyan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mahāsi Sayādaw, the Burmese treatise on insight meditation (Manual of Insight), two volumes, 1944"@en, "Mahāsi Sayādaw, die birmanische Abhandlung über die Einsichtsmeditation (Handbuch der Einsicht), zwei Bände, 1944"@de ;
    skos:note "The work the registration names. Of it, chapter V was read in English as pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation. The other chapters, which the author's preface says carry the references to the Pali texts, commentaries and subcommentaries, are unopened: the Burmese is in a language the run does not read, and an English translation of the whole under the title Manual of Insight was not found on archive.org, where that title belongs to Ledi Sayādaw's Vipassanā Dīpanī. Whether a full English translation is reachable elsewhere was not searched."@en .

pmw:LediManualsOfBuddhism a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism (dīpanī)"@en, "Ledi Sayādaw, die Handbücher des Buddhismus (dīpanī)"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: archive.org carries the English collection as athemanualsofbuddhismmahatheraledisayadaw_87_o and LediSayadawManualOfBuddhism, and the Vipassanā Dīpanī in U Ñāṇa's English as the-manual-of-insight (BPS Wheel 31/32). Ledi is the earlier station of the movement and the place to ask whether the bare-insight route and the marks of the stages entered here from Mahāsi already stand a generation before him."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:ModernVipassana a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Modern vipassanā movement"@en, "Moderne Vipassanā-Bewegung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The twentieth century Burmese reform transmission of insight practice as a lay technique detachable from monastic life, taught in fixed courses."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered separately from the canon and the commentaries because it is a distinct transmission with its own founders and manuals, and because its relation to the older material, in particular its treatment of absorption, is contested inside the tradition itself."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is printed in the works. The treatise takes the seven purifications of Buddhaghosa's handbook as its frame, names its knowledges with the handbook's names, and quotes the handbook, its great subcommentary, the Paṭisambhidāmagga, the Kathāvatthu and Majjhima commentaries and the Milindapañha at the places it needs them; the biography names the author's teacher, U Nārada of Thaton, the first Mingun Jetavan Sayādaw. Everything here that agrees with traditions/theravada.ttl and traditions/theravada-vism-samadhi.ttl is reception along that route and counts once. What the station added is entered as a pm:Reworking where the received claim stands as a node."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. For the treatise the text is the author's own Pali, printed with Nyanaponika's English in 1965; the English is cited for the page a reader finds and the Pali for the wording. For the two discourses only the English of the Rangoon print of 1979 was read, so every node from them rests on one translation, and the author's own words in Burmese were not seen."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: three works read complete; six practices; nineteen stages and states in the order of the treatise; four further states the prerequisites point at; seven prerequisites; one yielding with a term and the outcome it names; three conditions of failure entered as their own nodes and two more among the prerequisites; five cautions; two statements about the practitioner's body; and one reworking, each at a page."@en ;
    skos:note "Two counts, kept apart as SPEC §15 asks. Signs of success announced to the practitioner in advance, across the three works: nineteen — the many painful feelings and the mental images of a dagoba, a monk or a cloud at the knowledge of conditionality; the light, the rapture that lifts the body, the tranquillity, the happiness, the faith, the balanced energy and the equanimity of the early knowledge of arising and passing away; the vanishing of shape and the feeling of having lost the insight at dissolution; the fear, the insipidity and the disgust; the pains and the unwillingness to keep a posture at desire for deliverance; the severe pains and the restlessness at re-observation; their absence and the unforced noting for two or three hours at equanimity; the two or three acts of noting in rapid succession before the path; the flood of faith and rapture for one or two hours after it; the sitting posture that stays firm during fruition; the thrill or chill through the back; and the sounds that startle. Conditions of failure stated in advance: five — concentration short of perfection, under which equanimity only repeats and fruition does not come (pmc:VipassanaSamadhiAparipunna); faculties not mature, under which the higher path does not come in one, two or three days (pmc:VipassanaIndriyaAparipakka); perfections not developed, under which the progress stops at equanimity (pmc:VipassanaParamiAparipunna); the posture changed whenever a pain arises, under which concentration and with it the path are not had (pmc:VipassanaIriyapathaKhanti); and a striving for a higher path without a fixed period, under which the lower fruition cannot be re-entered (pmc:VipassanaKalaparicchedaUparimagga). Each of the five names a condition the practitioner cannot inspect except by the outcome it is to explain, which is the finding about the count."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Manual of Insight; Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation, pmw:VipassanaShuniKyan, pmw:LediManualsOfBuddhism ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965)" .

#
# The practices
#

pmp:VipassanaSallakkhana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Noting (sallakkhaṇa): marking each bodily and mental process at the six doors as it occurs"@en, "Bemerken (sallakkhaṇa): jeden körperlichen und geistigen Vorgang an den sechs Toren im Auftreten markieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one practice of the three works, of which every other entry here is a stage or a condition. The meditator notices, according to their specific and general characteristics, the bodily and mental processes that become evident at the six sense doors, and since that is at first impossible he begins with what is distinct: the touch of the sitting posture, noted as sitting, touching, and the rise and fall of the abdomen. Whatever arises in between — a thought, a feeling, an adjustment of the body — is noticed and the attention returns to the basic object. The discourses spell the method out to the swallowing of saliva, the lifting of a cup and each morsel of a meal, and require it from waking to falling asleep without relaxation."@en ;
    skos:note "Nyanaponika's note gives the Pali sallakkhaṇa, from sallakkheti, to mark clearly, and says that noticing is the same as the bare attention of his own book. The treatise says of its knowledges that they come from simple noticing and not from reasoning, and returns to the point at almost every stage: the reflection that arises between acts of noticing is itself to be noticed and not followed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 1-3 and n. 15 at p. 28; Pali text pp. 39-40", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 1-34", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 11-24" .

pmp:VipassanaUdaraUnnamanaOnamana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The rising and falling of the abdomen as the basic object"@en, "Das Heben und Senken der Bauchdecke als Grundgegenstand"@de ;
    skos:definition "The basic object of the method. While sitting, the meditator keeps the mind (not the eyes) on the abdomen and notes each upward movement as rising and each downward one as falling, as it occurs, without changing the natural breath and without repeating the words aloud; if the movement is not clear, both hands are placed on the abdomen. The treatise reads the movement as the wind element in its three aspects of stiffening, vibrating and pushing and pulling, and calls it the basic object of mindfulness in this practice."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator's note to the treatise says that this attention to the abdomen was introduced into the methodical practice of insight by the author himself, and allows the breath of the traditional mindfulness of breathing in its place. The appendix to Practical Insight Meditation spends its pages defending the object against the objection that the texts do not name it, by placing the movement under the wind element, the corporeality group, the tactile object and the truth of suffering. A practice whose author has to argue that it is in the canon is one the canon does not name, which is the reason this node carries the translator's note."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 2-3 and n. 4 at p. 27; Pali text pp. 39-40", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 13-15", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 12-13 and appendix pp. 58-60" .

pmp:VipassanaCankamana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SatipatthanaVipassanaMeditation, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Walking noted in one, two and three sections"@en, "Gehen, in einem, zwei und drei Abschnitten bemerkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Walking is noted as right step, left step or walking, walking when quick, and when slow in two sections, lifting, putting, and after two or three days in three, lifting, pushing, putting. The next step is not to begin until the first has ended. All movements are to be made slowly, as by a weak sick man or a man with lumbago, so that mindfulness can keep pace; the meditator is to behave as if blind and deaf."@en ;
    skos:note "The discourse sets a schedule here and nowhere else in the basic practice: two sections for the first two or three days, then three."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Satipatthana Vipassana Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 22-25", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 17-18" .

pmp:VipassanaPhalasamapattiAdhitthana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Resolving on the attainment of fruition: how soon, how often and for how long"@en, "Entschluss zur Erreichung der Frucht: wie bald, wie oft und wie lange"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the first path the meditator sets his mind on re-entering fruition, on entering it quickly and on abiding in it a long time, making the resolve before he begins to notice. The noticing then runs again from the knowledge of arising and passing away to equanimity, and with practice reaches equanimity after four or five acts of noticing. The same device of a resolve fixed in advance is used to review the knowledges one by one for a set time, and to set a period during which the meditator renounces the lower fruition in order to strive for a higher path."@en ;
    skos:note "The resolve is the one instrument in the three works by which the practitioner states an outcome before it occurs and then sees whether it occurs, and the works use it so: a stated duration that the fruition is said to keep, a stated knowledge that is said to stay for the stated time and give way to the next when it expires. That is the place where a protocol on this transmission would start."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), p. 24; Pali text p. 56", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 46-50" .

#
# The stages, in the order of the treatise
#

pmc:VipassanaSuddhavipassanayanika a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Two vehicles, and the one that goes by bare insight without absorption"@en, "Zwei Fahrzeuge, und das eine, das in bloßer Einsicht ohne Vertiefung geht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The treatise divides meditators into one who first develops tranquillity, establishes himself in access or full concentration and then contemplates the five groups of grasping (samatha-yānika), and one who contemplates the five groups without developing either concentration (suddha-vipassanā-yānika). It addresses itself to the second and supports him from the Papañcasūdanī on the Dhammadāyāda Sutta, from the Visuddhimagga, which says that one whose vehicle is pure insight contemplates the four elements, and from the Susīma Sutta."@en ;
    skos:note "The appendix of Practical Insight Meditation returns to the point under its own heading, insight meditation without prior jhāna, and grounds it in the commentarial phrase about the man who, terror-stricken at how rarely a Buddha's dispensation is met, begins insight without awaiting full concentration, on access alone. The registration names this treatment of absorption as contested inside the tradition; the three works read carry only the one side, and the dispute is not entered because its other side was not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 1-2; Pali text pp. 39-40", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), appendix pp. 61-63" .

pmc:VipassanaKhanikasamadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha ;
    rdfs:label "Momentary concentration counted as access and as purification of mind"@en, "Augenblickssammlung, als Zugang und als Reinigung des Geistes gezählt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Purification of mind for the bare-insight meditator is the concentration lasting a moment (khaṇika-samādhi) that arises in uninterrupted succession on each object noticed once the noticing has become strong and the stray thought is noticed at its arising and does not return. The treatise quotes the great subcommentary on the mindfulness of breathing for momentary unification of mind, glosses its four phrases, and then raises the objection itself: do the commentaries not say that purification of mind is access and absorption concentration only? Its answer is that momentary concentration is included in access, because it suppresses the hindrances as access does and stands in the neighbourhood of the path concentration; otherwise purification of mind could not come about for the one who has made bare insight his vehicle."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the argument and it runs backwards: the reading is required because the route would otherwise be impossible. The same paragraph says that the likeness of momentary to absorbed concentration becomes evident only when insight reaches its culmination, so the claim that the stage has been reached is checkable, by the treatise's own account, only from the end of the course. The holding carries the handbook's own threshold for absorption at pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 3-5; Pali text pp. 41-42" .

pmc:VipassanaNamarupaParicchedaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The analytical knowledge of body and mind, and purification of view"@en, "Die unterscheidende Kenntnis von Körper und Geist, und die Reinigung der Ansicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "With purification of mind the meditator comes to know, in each act of noticing and not by reasoning, that the rising is one process and the falling another, that the knowing of the rising is another again, and that the eye, the visible object, the seeing and the knowing of it are four. When this matures he understands that at the moment of breathing in there is just the rising and the knowing of it and no self besides, and that the conventional words being, person, man and woman refer to that pair alone. That is purification of view."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 5-7; Pali text pp. 42-43", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 25-26" .

pmc:VipassanaPaccayaPariggahaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge of conditionality, with its pains and its mental images"@en, "Die Kenntnis der Bedingtheit, mit ihren Schmerzen und ihren Vorstellungsbildern"@de ;
    skos:definition "When purification of view matures the condition of each process becomes evident: the intention to bend before the bending, the initial attention before the stray thought, which does not arise if that attention is noticed; seeing only when eye and object are present. At this time, the treatise says, the meditator will generally experience many and various painful feelings arising in his body, one after another in different places, and many mental images of various shapes — a dagoba, a monk, a man, a house, a tree, a park, a heavenly mansion, a cloud — each followed by another; of both he perceives only the arising and not the dissolution. Between acts of noticing he also comes to know by inference that body and mind continue because of ignorance, craving and kamma. That is purification by overcoming doubt."@en ;
    skos:note "The pains and the images are the first marks the treatise attaches to a stage, and they are told to the reader in advance. Counted with the signs of success at the tradition instance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 7-8; Pali text pp. 43-44", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 26-29" .

pmc:VipassanaSammasanaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge of comprehension, by direct experience and by inference"@en, "Die Kenntnis des Erfassens, durch unmittelbare Erfahrung und durch Schluss"@de ;
    skos:definition "The meditator now discerns the beginning, middle and end of each object and sees each arise only after the last has ceased; a painful feeling noticed twice, thrice or more grows less and ceases, and an image moves, shrinks and vanishes. Seeing each object destroyed as it is noticed he comprehends it as impermanent, as painful in breaking up after arising, and as without a master; having seen this once or several times he extends it by inference to all processes past, present and future, which the treatise calls comprehension by inference and supports from the Paṭisambhidāmagga and the commentaries to the Kathāvatthu and the Majjhima."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation adds that such inferential comprehension comes often to one who yields to reflection, that excessive reflecting is an impediment to insight, and that no attention should be given to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 8-9; Pali text pp. 44-46", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 29-31" .

pmc:VipassanaTarunaUdayabbayaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The early knowledge of arising and passing away, and the ten things that come with it"@en, "Die frühe Kenntnis von Entstehen und Vergehen, und die zehn Dinge, die mit ihr kommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the meditator can keep exclusively to the present process, a brilliant light appears to him, like a lamp, lightning, the moon or the sun, for a moment or longer. With it arise strong mindfulness, in which the processes seem to come to the noticing of themselves; keen knowledge, as of a bamboo sprout cut with a sharp knife; strong faith, with the wish to proclaim the teaching, to advise friends and relatives to meditate and grateful remembrance of the teacher; rapture in its five grades, in which the body feels risen into the air or seated on an air cushion; tranquillity with agility, pliancy and wieldiness; a sublime happiness he wants to tell others about; energy neither lax nor tense; equanimity; and a subtle attachment that takes all this for the delight of meditation. Then the meditator believes that he has attained the path and fruition and finished the task. That belief is the corruption of mistaking what is not the path for the path, and even without it the delight in these things is a corruption, so that the knowledge, however quick, is the early or weak knowledge of arising and passing away."@en ;
    skos:note "The list differs in names and order from the handbook's ten imperfections at Visuddhimagga XX.105, as Nyanaponika's note says, and the Pali shows where: the faith here carries the wish to teach, to send friends and relatives to meditation and to remember the instructor's help, which the treatise writes for a meditation centre. The same ten are entered from the handbook at pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight and at the four nodes beside it in traditions/theravada-vism-samadhi.ttl; the agreement is reception and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Eight signs of success in one passage, all announced, and the warning against them in the same passage. Practical Insight Meditation adds tears, the feeling of being on a swing and the wondering whether one is giddy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 9-13; Pali text pp. 46-48", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 31-33" .

pmc:VipassanaMaggamaggaNanadassana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The decision that the light is not the path, reached by oneself or on being told"@en, "Die Entscheidung, dass das Licht nicht der Weg ist, selbst gefunden oder gesagt bekommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "While noticing, the meditator comes to the decision that the light and the other things experienced are not the path, that delight in them is a corruption, and that continuous noticing of the object as it becomes evident is alone the way of insight, and that he must go on noticing. The treatise says that he reaches it either by himself or through instructions from someone else (sayam eva vā parato vā sutvā). That decision is purification by knowledge and vision of what is path and not-path."@en ;
    skos:note "The second route matters for anyone reading the stages as reports. A stage that can be reached by being told what it consists in cannot be told apart, from the outside, from having been told; see the caution at pmc:VipassanaNanaPubbajananaCautela, where the same author draws the consequence for the whole sequence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), p. 13; Pali text p. 48", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 33-34" .

pmc:VipassanaBalavaUdayabbayaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The mature knowledge of arising and passing away, free of the corruptions"@en, "Die reife Kenntnis von Entstehen und Vergehen, frei von den Verunreinigungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Going on noticing, the meditator passes beyond the light, rapture, tranquillity, happiness and attachment, and his knowledge stays with the arising and passing away alone: at each act of noticing he sees the object arise and disappear instantly and disappear where it arose without moving to another place. This is the beginning of purification by knowledge and vision of the course of practice, which extends to adaptation knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation warns at this stage that the meditator, satisfied, may think this the best that can be attained and pause to relax, and tells him not to."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 13-14; Pali text p. 48", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 33-34" .

pmc:VipassanaBhangaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge of dissolution, and the impression of having lost the insight"@en, "Die Kenntnis der Auflösung, und der Eindruck, die Einsicht verloren zu haben"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the knowledge of arising and passing away matures, neither the arising nor the middle nor the continuity of the processes is apparent, nor the shape of hand, foot, face or body, but only their ceasing: in noticing the rising of the abdomen only its vanishing appears, and the object seems absent and the noticing to have lost contact with it. Then the meditator sees object and noticing dissolve together, pair by pair, like a mirage, like bubbles under heavy rain, like lamps blown out at a shrine. At this stage a meditator may think he has lost the insight, and the treatise says he should not think so: his mind formerly delighted in shapes and features, and will soon delight in their cessation."@en ;
    skos:note "The feeling of failure is here declared in advance to be a sign of progress. Practical Insight Meditation goes further: some meditators feel their eyesight is getting poorer or that they are giddy, and it is not so; at that time they should relax and stop contemplating, and the noticing continues of itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 14-16; Pali text pp. 49-50", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 34-36" .

pmc:VipassanaBhayaAdinavaNibbidaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledges of fearfulness, misery and disgust"@en, "Die Kenntnisse der Furchtbarkeit, des Elends und des Überdrusses"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the dissolution of everything noticed the meditator infers that all formations have broken up and will break up, and they appear to him as fearful, his mind gripped by fear and seeming helpless; then, noticing on, they appear insipid, without a vitalizing factor and unsatisfying; then his mind finds no delight in them, is discontented and listless, and inclines only to Nibbāna, and between acts of noticing he thinks that the ceasing of all formations alone is happiness. The treatise says the listlessness is not dissatisfaction with meditation but the knowledge of disgust."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation calls the dejection that comes with the fear a sign of the progress of insight, and adds the one statement in the three works that a mental state at this stage can go wrong: if the meditator fails to contemplate for some time, grief asserts itself and a fear overpowers him that is not associated with insight. That is entered as a caution at pmc:VipassanaBhayaAsallakkhanaCautela."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 16-17; Pali text pp. 50-51", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 36-38" .

pmc:VipassanaMuncitukamyataNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge of desire for deliverance, with pains and an unwillingness to keep a posture"@en, "Die Kenntnis des Wunsches nach Befreiung, mit Schmerzen und dem Unwillen, eine Haltung zu halten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Disgusted with every formation noticed, the meditator desires to be delivered from them. At that time, the treatise says, usually various painful feelings arise in his body and an unwillingness to remain long in one posture, and where they do not arise the comfortless nature of the formations is more evident than ever; between acts of noticing he longs to be free of them, and the noticing seems to shrink from its object."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation reports that some meditators at this point actually stop noticing, find that the processes go on and are known of themselves, and conclude with delight that deliverance does not come by stopping; those who cannot reason so continue when satisfied with their teacher's explanation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), p. 17; Pali text p. 51", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 38-39" .

pmc:VipassanaPatisankhaDukkhavedana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Re-observation: severe pains and restlessness of posture, to be sat through motionless"@en, "Wiederbetrachtung: heftige Schmerzen und Unruhe der Haltung, die regungslos auszusitzen sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Desiring to escape the formations, the meditator notices them again with stronger effort (paṭisaṅkhā-ñāṇa), and the three characteristics, the painful one above all, become distinct. At this stage, the treatise says, there will usually arise in his body various pains that are severe, sharp and of growing intensity, so that the whole bodily and mental system seems an unbearable mass of sickness, and a restlessness that makes him unable to keep one posture for any length of time. That restlessness merely shows the unbearable nature of the formations, and though he wants to change his posture he should not give in to the wish but remain motionless longer in the same posture and go on noticing; so he overcomes it. His knowledge is now strong enough that a pain firmly noticed ceases at once, or is seen to dissolve moment by moment, and if noticed without abandoning the practice it ceases entirely before long, and when it ceases so it ceases for good and does not arise again. He will still think his insight is not clear, and should notice that thought too; persevering as the time passes in minutes, hours and days, he overcomes the pains, the restlessness and that thought."@en ;
    skos:note "What this station adds to the handbook it takes the stage from. The Visuddhimagga's knowledge of contemplation of reflection, entered at pmc:PatisankhanupassanaNana, attributes the three characteristics to the formations under forty-odd headings in order to contrive the means of deliverance, and says nothing in either translation read of bodily pain, of a posture that cannot be kept or of an instruction to stay still. The treatise attaches to the same stage a felt bodily mark, a behavioural mark and an instruction, and a claim about the pain that is checkable by the person who has it: firmly noticed, it ceases and does not come back. The reworking is at pmc:VipassanaPatisankhaReworking."@en ;
    skos:note "The Pali has the times as minita, ghaṭikā and divasa, which Nyanaponika renders minutes, hours and days."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 18-19; Pali text pp. 51-52", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 39-40" .

pmc:VipassanaSankharupekkhaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Equanimity about formations: noticing that runs of itself for two or three hours, without pain"@en, "Gleichmut gegenüber den Gebilden: ein Bemerken, das von selbst zwei oder drei Stunden läuft, ohne Schmerz"@de ;
    skos:definition "When re-observation matures, the noticing proceeds of itself, as if borne onward, and no effort is needed to keep the formations before the mind or to understand them. Fear, misery, disgust, desire for deliverance and dissatisfaction no longer arise; a pain, if it arises, brings no grief, and generally at this stage pains are absent altogether; the strong joy of the earlier stage does not arise either, and pleasant and unpleasant objects are taken alike. If the meditator resumes with the resolve to note vigorously, the noticing before long runs in a continuous flow for a long time without deliberate effort, even for two or three hours without interruption; and the mind sent out to other objects refuses to go, or soon returns."@en ;
    skos:note "The duration is the treatise's own figure for the stage, in the Pali dvattighaṭikā, two or three ghaṭikā, which Nyanaponika renders hours. Practical Insight Meditation gives the same two or three hours and adds a time-dependent failure: if the meditator does not develop sufficient insight to gain the path within two or three hours, concentration becomes slack and reflection sets in, and some meditators progress and fall back several times; it compares the movement to a bird released from a ship that returns while it sees no land."@en ;
    skos:note "Two signs announced in advance, the duration and the absence of pain, and one of them is an absence the practitioner can observe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 19-20; Pali text pp. 52-54", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 40-43" .

pmc:VipassanaVutthanagaminiMaggaphala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Insight leading to emergence, adaptation, maturity, path and fruition, in an instant"@en, "Zum Auftauchen führende Einsicht, Anpassung, Reife, Pfad und Frucht, in einem Augenblick"@de ;
    skos:definition "When equanimity reaches its culmination it understands one formation as impermanent or painful or without self by seeing its dissolution, and that act of noticing, more lucid than any before it, occurs two or three times or more in rapid succession: insight leading to emergence, whose last member is adaptation. Immediately after it the consciousness leaps into Nibbāna, the cessation of all formations, and the stilling of all formations appears: maturity knowledge, path knowledge and fruition knowledge follow one another, and the three together last no longer than a single thought of noticing. The treatise supports the moment from the discourses on the arising of the vision of truth and from the Milindapañha's consciousness that passes beyond occurrence and alights on non-occurrence."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation gives the experience as those who have realized Nibbāna describe it, and gives eleven forms: object and noticing cease altogether, are cut off like a vine, fall off like a load, break away, are freed as from a prison, are blown out like a candle, give way as darkness to light, are released from an embroilment, sink as in water, stop as a runner stopped by a push. Eleven descriptions of one event that lasts an instant is a list a meditator can match after the fact, and the work offers no mark by which a cessation that did not occur would be told from one that did."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 20-22; Pali text pp. 54-55", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 43-44" .

pmc:VipassanaPaccavekkhanaNana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewing the path, the fruition and Nibbāna, and in some the defilements"@en, "Rückschau auf Pfad, Frucht und Nibbāna, und bei manchen auf die Befleckungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the instant the meditator discerns that the insight leading to emergence came with very rapid noticing and that the path consciousness entered cessation after the last act of noticing; that the mind abode in cessation in the interval; and that the object just experienced is void of all formations. Some meditators, but not all, review the defilements. Resuming the noticing, the processes appear coarse again, because for the noble disciple the knowledge of arising and passing away usually arises first."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation adds that the well informed say inwardly, I have now realized Nibbāna and attained the fruition of stream-entry, and that such clear knowledge is evident to one who has studied the scriptures or heard sermons on the subject; and that the attainer feels his temperament and attitude changed and his faith in the three gems firm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 22-23; Pali text pp. 55-56", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 44-45" .

pmc:VipassanaMaggaphalaPitiOgha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "After the path: faith, rapture and tranquillity that flood the body for one or two hours and stop the noticing"@en, "Nach dem Pfad: Vertrauen, Freude und Ruhe, die den Körper ein bis zwei Stunden durchfluten und das Bemerken anhalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "When some meditators emerge from path and fruition, great faith, happiness, rapture and tranquillity flood the whole body, and they cannot notice anything distinctly however hard they try; the serene state lasts one hour, two hours or more without break, they feel as if in a wide open space suffused with radiance, and they say they have never felt such happiness before. After two or three hours it fades, they can notice again, and the knowledge of arising and passing away appears first."@en ;
    skos:note "Practical Insight Meditation puts the waning at some hours or days, and adds that some, relieved of a great burden, do not wish to go on contemplating. The Pali counts in ghaṭikā, one, two and two or three."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), pp. 23-24; Pali text p. 56", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 45-46" .

pmc:VipassanaPhalasamapatti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The attainment of fruition, entered at will and held for the time resolved, even while walking or eating"@en, "Die Erreichung der Frucht, nach Willen betreten und für die beschlossene Zeit gehalten, auch beim Gehen oder Essen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Noticing on, the meditator reaches equanimity again; if his concentration is short of perfection it only repeats, and if it is perfect, the fruition consciousness of the first path reaches the cessation of formations by way of the attainment of fruition, exactly as at the first attainment, differing only in its capacity to last. He should resolve to repeat it, to enter it quickly and to abide in it, for five, ten, fifteen or thirty minutes or an hour or more; with skill, equanimity comes after four or five acts of noticing, the mind is absorbed again and again, even while walking up and down or taking a meal, and the attainment lasts for the time resolved. During it the mind abides in cessation alone and is aware of nothing else."@en ;
    skos:note "Nyanaponika's English prints six minutes where the Pali has pañca-minita, five. Practical Insight Meditation adds the duration in these days of an hour, two or three, the statement that fruition ends only when the meditator wishes to terminate it, and a mark visible to another person: if it comes while he is sitting, his posture stays firm without bending or sagging."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity stated with a duration fixed in advance by the practitioner and a mark another can see. It is the most examinable claim in the three works, and none of them reports a case in which the resolved time was not kept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), p. 24; Pali text pp. 56-57", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 46-47" .

pmc:VipassanaUparimaggaBhavana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisuddhinanaKatha, pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "The higher paths: equanimity regained in a day or an hour, the path withheld while the faculties are unripe"@en, "Die höheren Pfade: Gleichmut in einem Tag oder einer Stunde wiedergewonnen, der Pfad vorenthalten, solange die Fähigkeiten unreif sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Skilled in fruition, the meditator resolves on the higher paths and notices at the six doors as before. The development is harder than re-entering fruition, because insight has to be developed anew, but not as hard as the first time: in a single day, or even in a single hour, he can gain equanimity. The treatise marks the statement as an inference from the usual experience of present-day persons who had to be guided from the start and were not of particularly strong intelligence. If the five spiritual faculties are not mature, equanimity merely repeats, and though he can enter his lower fruition several times in an hour, he cannot attain the next path within one, two, three or more days; if he turns his mind to the lower fruition, he reaches it in two or three minutes. When the faculties are mature, the higher path and fruition arise after equanimity as before, and so up to arahantship."@en ;
    skos:note "The treatise is the only one of the three works to say on what its figures rest, and it says so here: on the persons usually met with, and applied to similar persons by inference. Practical Insight Meditation puts the same equanimity at possibly a day to months or years for the second path, and says that the third path is hard to reach for a long time because it requires fully developed concentration and not virtue alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, The Progress of Insight (Visuddhiñāṇakathā), trans. Nyanaponika Thera, with the Pali text (Kandy: The Forest Hermitage, 1965), p. 25; Pali text p. 57", "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 49-52" .

pmc:VipassanaNanaAdhitthanaPaccavekkhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PracticalInsightMeditation ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewing the knowledges by resolve: each stays for the time fixed, and the next rises when it expires"@en, "Rückschau auf die Kenntnisse durch Entschluss: jede bleibt für die festgesetzte Zeit, und die nächste steigt auf, wenn sie abläuft"@de ;
    skos:definition "One who passed through fear, misery, disgust and desire for deliverance without seeing them clearly should review each for a fixed time, for half an hour or an hour, resolving on the knowledge of arising and passing away; during that period it remains and there is no further progress, and when the period expires the knowledge of dissolution arises by itself, or else he gives heed to dissolution with a resolve that it stay for a certain time. So each knowledge he aspires to arises during the stipulated time and on its expiration the next arises as if it were a barometric rise; the review is repeated until he is satisfied. To a very ardent meditator, it says, equanimity and fruition may come in a few moments, and one well matured can attain fruition while walking or having a meal."@en ;
    skos:note "A claim with its own clock. The practitioner fixes the period and the knowledge, and the text says what happens at the end of it, so that a knowledge which stayed past the period, or gave way before it, would be a case against the claim; the text's only provision for a next knowledge that does not come by itself is to turn the attention to it deliberately, which it treats as part of the procedure and not as a failure. That the practitioner has been told beforehand what each knowledge feels like is the condition that weakens any case, and the same work says so at pmc:VipassanaNanaPubbajananaCautela."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ModernVipassana ;
    dcterms:source "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Practical Insight Meditation, trans. U Pe Thin and Myanaung U Tin (Rangoon: Department of Religious Affairs, 1979), pp. 47-49" .

# ANKER-W3
